# Hot top-level loop in a program that opens with `use`.
#
# The top-level chunk compiles all-or-nothing: one opcode the emitter rejects
# disqualifies the entire main body, and everything in it falls back to the
# register VM. USE_LIB used to be such an opcode, so a single `use` line - which
# most real programs start with - cost the whole program its native compilation.
# The import itself is trivial; what it used to cost was everything after it.
#
# Measured with tools/sura_ab_bench.ps1 -Jit, 10 interleaved rounds:
#   USE_LIB bailing  : 15.26 ms   (top level compiled 0 opcodes)
#   USE_LIB emitted  :  2.63 ms   (top level compiled 15 opcodes)
#   10/10 rounds, 5.8x
#
# Run with --jit and check the [JIT-OPS] line: USE_LIB must appear, and so must
# HALT - their absence means the top level stopped compiling and this benchmark
# will show it as a ~6x slowdown.

use math

runs is 5
total is 0

repeat runs do
  t0 is clock()
  acc is 0
  i is 1
  while i < 400000 do
    acc is acc + (i * 3) - (i / 2)
    i is i + 1
  end
  t1 is clock()
  total is total + (t1 - t0) * 1000
end

avg is total / runs

# Pinned so a miscompiled top level shows up as a wrong answer rather than only
# as a timing change.
assert_eq(acc, 199999500000)

print "checksum: {acc}"
print "avg ({runs} runs): {avg} ms"
